            </section>
            <section>
               <title value="Hospital course note"/>
               <code>
                  <coding>
                     <system value="http://loinc.org"/>
                     <code value="8648-8"/>
                     <display value="Hospital course note"/>
                  </coding>
               </code>
               <text>
                  <status value="generated"/>
                  <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">Dear colleague, thank you for referring this 78-year-old female patient, admitted on 17/09/1999 with an acute exacerbation of her known chronic obstructive pulmonary disease. On admission, she presented with increased dyspnoea, productive cough, and reduced oxygen saturation. Clinical examination confirmed bilateral expiratory wheezing. Chest X-ray showed pulmonary hyperinflation without evidence of pneumonia or cardiac decompensation. Arterial blood gas analysis revealed hypoxaemia with mild hypercapnia. Blood tests showed mild inflammatory markers. She was treated with nebulised bronchodilators, systemic corticosteroids, supplemental oxygen therapy, and monitored closely with pulse oximetry. She responded well to treatment with significant symptomatic improvement. She is discharged in stable condition with instructions for inhaler technique and exacerbation action plan. Please continue outpatient COPD management and consider pulmonary rehabilitation referral.</div>
               </text>
            </section>
